{"clock":2,"ops":[{"kind":"set-notes","author":"ee004d31386b76440a74651c79871f156633d4ff","time":1793611837,"data":{"notes":"## git-board v0.12.0\n\nThe project file as forms, field homes per forge, and a pass over the whole UI.\n\n### Settings: the project file as forms\n- `.git-board.toml` is now edited through forms: one Settings tab per section (Fields, Labels, Views, Sprints, Roles, Branches, Cycle, Repos, Attachments, Release). Each tab saves only its own section, and keeps what its form doesn't show.\n- Each card switches between its form and its own TOML. The raw-file tab (\"The file\") saves the same way as the others.\n- **Fields** is a list plus an editor for one field:\n  - The form follows the type. A date, person, issue or sprint field has no values, and saving drops any values the file had for it.\n  - The editor guesses the type from the values (numbers, free text) and says what it guessed.\n  - Values reorder by drag or Alt+←/→.\n  - Past twelve fields, the list gets a filter.\n  - A banner appears when the file changes on disk, and errors show on the rows.\n- Settings opens on **Sync**. The Overview is gone: each menu item shows its own state (\"23 labels\", \"off on this server\").\n- **Labels** has three tabs: Plain labels, On GitHub, and Lint. Lint shows one line per field, with a link to the issues concerned. Every namespace found in the issues counts as a field, and the undeclared ones are grouped.\n- On a phone, Settings has a dropdown menu, and the field list and editor are separate screens.\n\n### Where a field lives on each forge\n- New `[forges.\u003cname\u003e.fields.\u003cfield\u003e]` tables say where a field lives on that forge:\n  - `to` is one of label, scoped-label, project, assignees, milestone, iteration, weight, due-date, sub-issues or none.\n  - `as` says whether the label is namespaced or plain.\n  - `field` names the Project column.\n  - `values` holds the value renames on that forge.\n\n  Each table holds only what differs from the type's default.\n- Defaults follow the type:\n  - a person field uses the forge's assignees;\n  - an issue field uses GitHub sub-issues;\n  - an iteration uses the GitLab milestone, and a date the GitLab due date;\n  - anything else is a namespaced label.\n- A mapping that doesn't fit its type or forge stops the file from loading, and the error names the table.\n- Fields take `aliases`, so a retired value is read as its replacement (`could = \"couldhave\"`).\n- The old `[fields.X] project` map keeps working. Lint suggests moving it (check `project-key`).\n- **Migration:** changing a mapping moves nothing until you migrate. `git board sync --migrate \u003cfield\u003e` (or the Settings › Forges fields page) previews the counts, then moves the values and takes the old labels off the forge.\n- The Forge fields page shows the forge found in the git remotes, even when the file declares none.\n\n### UI review\n- **Layout:**\n  - hints read as sentences;\n  - names in the Fields list are no longer cut off;\n  - config cards stay in their column;\n  - the Sync table scrolls on a phone, and the forge panel wraps;\n  - dialogs scroll inside the screen;\n  - the Columns menu fits the screen.\n- **One place per thing:**\n  - Sync is the one place to sync from; Work's Send all and Check first are gone;\n  - a single way to open the command palette (the header 🔍);\n  - Close the issue appears only once on the issue page;\n  - the avatar menu is simpler.\n- **Work:** what waits for the forges is grouped by what it waits for, and each row shows its own result.\n- **New issue:** each input follows its field's type (a date picker for dates), with short placeholder examples.\n- **Issue list:** a half-typed filter term is ignored instead of emptying the list. A column \"On the forge\" shows \"GitHub #N\".\n- **Board:** on a phone, the board shows one lane per screen.\n- **Release page:** now in its two columns, without empty sections. Release settings count the binaries.\n- **Insights:**\n  - lead time leaves out issues opened and closed in the same minute at import;\n  - open issues already at done turn into a \"close it?\" nudge;\n  - the numbers can be shown as a table.\n- **Smaller fixes:**\n  - focus outlines use the text color;\n  - placeholders read as examples;\n  - a disabled Save says why;\n  - Attachments starts with whether uploads are on for this server;\n  - Branches marks kinds the kind field doesn't have;\n  - the forge's number shows beside its logo.\n\n### Lint\n- New check, **near-value**: `risk:-normal` → \"did you mean risk:normal?\". An unknown value that differs only by case or punctuation names the value it meant (`1.5` is not taken for `15`).\n\n### Other\n- A missing token now says what to do: \"no token for github.com: set GITHUB_TOKEN … or store one with git's credential helper\".\n- The config API never answers `null`.\n- The docs site is published to GitHub Pages on every push to main.\n- Playwright end-to-end tests for the settings pages (`make web-e2e`).\n"}}]}